DMA-IOVA page-size stress selftest
==================================

This test exercises the DMA-IOVA allocation/link/sync/destroy API against a
real, driver-bound PCI function without changing its driver binding, DMA mask,
or device state, and without issuing any device command.  The module accepts
only coherent devices in a translating DMA-IOMMU domain.  It owns every folio
that it maps and destroys every successfully linked prefix before freeing it.

Use a quiet, dedicated test device: the target driver remains active and the
test intentionally perturbs its domain's IOVA allocator and IOTLB.  Run as
root, and identify the function explicitly:

  ./dma_iova_pgsize.sh --bdf 0000:03:00.0 --api current --order 9
  ./dma_iova_pgsize.sh --bdf 0000:03:00.0 --api strict --order 9

The immediate mode maps and destroys each mapping.  Churn keeps a bounded live
set and replaces a deterministic pseudo-random member.  Ramp retains mappings
until max-live, iterations, or the byte cap is reached.  The hard limits are
1 GiB live mapped bytes, 4096 live mappings, 4096 folios per mapping, and
100000 iterations.  Folio allocations use bounded, non-retrying reclaim.

Strict negative checks are safe and issue no device command:

  ./dma_iova_pgsize.sh --bdf 0000:03:00.0 --api strict \
          --negative misaligned --order 9
  ./dma_iova_pgsize.sh --bdf 0000:03:00.0 --api strict \
          --negative unsupported

The output is one JSON object.  For perf, configure once and place only the
synchronous trigger inside the measured interval:

  ./dma_iova_pgsize.sh --bdf 0000:03:00.0 --api strict --order 9 \
          --iterations 1000 --configure-only
  perf stat -x, -r 10 -e task-clock,cycles,instructions,branches,\
branch-misses,cache-misses -- ./dma_iova_pgsize.sh --run-only
  modprobe -r dma_iova_pgsize_selftest

The harness records the source-tree HEAD as kernel_commit when it is run from
a Git checkout.  Use --kernel-commit with the exact 40-character object name
when running an installed copy.

For IOMMU leaf-size attribution, take a bounded, kernel-formatted tracefs
snapshot around --run-only.  The following sequence first disables collection,
clears stale records, enables only the leaf event for the synchronous run, and
disables collection before copying the snapshot.  It temporarily controls the
global tracer, so use it only on the dedicated test system described above:

  tracefs=/sys/kernel/tracing
  leaf_enable=$tracefs/events/iommu/iommu_map_leaf/enable
  trace_stop()
  {
	  echo 0 > "$tracefs/tracing_on"
	  echo 0 > "$leaf_enable"
  }
  trap trace_stop EXIT
  trap 'exit 130' HUP INT TERM

  trace_stop
  : > "$tracefs/trace"
  echo 1 > "$leaf_enable"
  echo 1 > "$tracefs/tracing_on"
  run_status=0
  ./dma_iova_pgsize.sh --run-only > result.json || run_status=$?
  trace_stop
  cat "$tracefs/trace" > leaf-events.txt
  trap - EXIT HUP INT TERM
  test "$run_status" -eq 0

Then filter leaf-events.txt using domain_token, iova_start, and iova_size from
result.json:

  ./leaf_hist.py --domain <domain_token> \
	  --iova-start <base> --iova-size <len> \
	  --expected-mapped-bytes <expected_leaf_mapped_bytes> \
          --format json leaf-events.txt

The domain token is the stable, boot-local ``%p`` representation used by both
the module JSON and the kernel's tracefs formatter.  Do not substitute a perf
capture for this tracefs snapshot.  ``perf record`` records the raw domain
pointer field and ``perf script`` renders it in userspace, so its domain text
can differ from the kernel-formatted ``%p`` token reported by the module.  It
therefore cannot be exact-domain-filtered with domain_token.  The parser
requires an exact match so events from an overlapping IOVA range in another
domain cannot contaminate the histogram.  By default leaf_hist.py excludes
nonzero-status partial/error records.  Add
--include-nonzero-status when diagnosing unwind paths.  leaf_hist_test.py is
the self-contained parser regression test run by kselftest.  Leaf records are
emitted when entries are installed, before the complete map transaction or its
final sync is known to have succeeded.  Always use expected-mapped-bytes for
geometry claims so a rolled-back prefix or incomplete capture fails closed.
Keep tracepoint collection separate from timed CPU comparisons because the
number of emitted records can differ substantially between mapping modes.
